<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Gorges</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Gorges are the narrow, steep-sided canyons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> between Fengjie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> and Yichang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>: the Qutang, Wu and Xiling gorges. For centuries they were the main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and the middle Yangtze, and controlled access to the lands of the ancient Ba people. The Three Gorges Dam, completed in the 2000s, flooded much of the gorges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-gorges.jpg" alt="Three Gorges" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tan Wei Liang Byorn · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qutang_Gorge_on_Changjiang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Three Gorges lie on the section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> known as the Chuanjiang, or river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, which runs from Jiangjin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> to Yichang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>. The gorges span 311 km, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baidicheng">Baidicheng</a> in the west to Nanjin Pass at Yichang in the east, with the Fengjie and Wushan counties of Chongqing and the Badong and Zigui counties of Hubei between them. In the past this stretch was the only waterway linking Sichuan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> with eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>Geologists generally hold that the mountain folding of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, including the Wu Mountains, came from the Yanshanian movement around 70 million years ago. As the crust kept rising the river cut deeper. The valleys are narrow where hard limestone resists erosion and broad where softer sandstone and shale give way. Among the named sights are Kuimen at the western entrance and the twelve peaks of the Wu Mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The scenery was written about early. In the Northern Wei period Li Daoyuan described the gorges in his Commentary on the Water Classic, and Yuan Shansong of the Eastern Jin wrote a Record of Yichuan's Landscape. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bai">Li Bai</a> wrote of them in Setting out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baidicheng">Baidicheng</a>, with monkeys howling on both banks as the boat sped past the mountains.</p><p>Advances in shipbuilding and navigation under the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties allowed regular traffic, but volumes stayed low until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> was resettled in the 1600s. By the late 1700s about 210,000,000 kg of grain went downstream each year, while boats were hauled upstream by hand. Passage was dangerous, and government and private projects of the 1700s and 1800s carved towpaths, removed rocks and set up pilot and lifeboat stations. Manual towing lasted in part until the 1970s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> proposed damming the gorges in 1919, but work on the Three Gorges project began only in 1994, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a> approved it on 3 April 1992 by 1767 votes of 2633. The dam stands at Sandouping in the middle of Xiling Gorge. Its reservoir relocated towns and villages and submerged many archaeological sites, hanging coffins and inscriptions; the Zhang Fei Temple was moved whole, Shibaozhai was ringed by a protective dam, and the Baiheliang hydrographic inscriptions are now seen in an underwater museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Three Gorges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gongsun Shu called himself the White Emperor, after the element of metal, which gave the name to Baidicheng in the <b>Three Gorges</b>, and issued iron coins.</blockquote><a href="/state/chengjia">Chengjia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ba were peoples of the eastern Sichuan basin and the <b>Three Gorges</b> of the Yangtze.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-ba">State of Ba</a></li></ul></section>
